WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.940
[Kate:] Welcome to the keynote session!

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:10.320
My name is Kate Moran and I'm a UX specialist
with Nielsen Norman Group.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:18.580
You may not be aware of this, but 2018 is
actually Nielsen Norman Group's 20 year anniversary.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:27.430
That is 20 years of research-based UX guidance.

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:32.910
We are so happy that you could be here with
us this week to help us celebrate and also

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:36.420
to be a part of this milestone with us.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:45.600
Now I hope you'll join me in welcoming the
UX guru himself, Dr. Jakob Nielsen.

00:00:51.139 --> 00:00:52.519
[Jakob:] Thank you very much, Kate.

00:00:52.519 --> 00:00:56.979
And thank you everybody for spending the beginning
of the evening with us.

00:00:56.979 --> 00:01:01.829
I want to talk not about what I think people
often talk about in these types of presentations,

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:07.060
which is what's new, what's leading edge
and what is the latest fad.

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:09.930
We do that a little bit at the conference
here as well.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:11.310
But that's not a lot.

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:14.219
I actually want to talk about what's not new.

00:01:14.219 --> 00:01:21.100
What are the things that really keep being
long-lasting in our field?

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:22.740
That's what I want to focus on.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:28.649
This means I'm not going to talk about these
things like particular screenshots, particular

00:01:28.649 --> 00:01:35.560
devices, particular services, dialog boxes,
interface designs, or things like that.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:42.399
Here are some of the older ones that I've
worked on or analyzed over the years.

00:01:42.399 --> 00:01:45.270
I just wanted to point out a few that are
interesting, historically speaking.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:51.299
The upper left was Apple's eWorld, which
was their attempt to make their own version

00:01:51.299 --> 00:01:54.289
of the internet or the web which didn't last
very long.

00:01:54.289 --> 00:01:57.680
Has anyone here actually used or had an eWorld account? Anybody?

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.540
I'm the only one. Okay! That shows you why it failed.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:08.170
Lower left is a Japanese device from NTT called
the Eggy.

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:15.280
I really used to love when I went to 
Tokyo and could go into the Docomo stores and look at all the fancy mobile devices they had.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.709
They had a lot of very specialized things
that you couldn't buy in the rest of the world.

00:02:20.709 --> 00:02:23.959
Today all the mobile phones are the same,
they all look like a little brick, so that

00:02:23.959 --> 00:02:27.560
part is not as exciting anymore.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:31.260
But what's interesting to look about this
device from 2001, this was a specialized device

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:32.830
for taking selfies.

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:36.659
It then could transmit to your friends.

00:02:36.659 --> 00:02:41.069
That is no longer a special device, it's just
a feature on your smartphone.

00:02:41.069 --> 00:02:46.640
What's interesting to look if you look closely
at the photo on this old device...

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:50.939
This shows you one of these immutable facts,
which is that people love to put little emojis

00:02:50.939 --> 00:02:54.549
or stickies on their selfie photos.

00:02:54.549 --> 00:02:59.020
That was true in 2001, it's true now, it's
probably going to be true for another 20 years or forever.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:02.860
That's the human nature and that's a lot of
what this is about.

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:08.320
So I want to start talking about one of the
early examples of a successful user experience project.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:14.340
This was from 1984, the Olympic Message System
developed by IBM for the Olympic Games that year.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:22.000
It was kiosks that were scattered around the
Olympic village with what we today would call voicemail.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.540
Back then, most people didn't have that.

00:03:25.549 --> 00:03:29.930
Particularly most of these athletes that came
from all over the world didn't have that.

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:34.230
So it was a special development project that
had to have this very easy, walk up and use

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:38.860
type of functionality because the athletes
were focused on their competitions and not

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:40.500
on learning an IBM system.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.480
It had to be really easy to use.

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:48.060
Some of the design challenges that they had
back then were that many had never used a

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:54.090
push-button telephone before, and it was a
very international system because the athletes

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:56.290
came from all different countries.

00:03:56.290 --> 00:04:01.640
They delivered this user interface in twelve
different languages, which even today is quite

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:05.320
impressive, very few designs ship in that
many languages from the beginning.

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:10.409
It also had to be supportive of a lot of different
issues -- I'll highlight one which is

00:04:10.409 --> 00:04:14.691
people coming from different countries, many
did not use the Latin character set, so they

00:04:14.691 --> 00:04:18.740
didn't know how to spell their names.

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:23.150
They knew their own name in their local language
and character set, but not in this alphabet.

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:25.950
Passwords were also in the Latin alphabet.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:31.800
So the solution to several of these problems was
that they recorded a video with a mime, which

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:35.000
takes care of the language problem right there.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:39.280
You can see this screenshot from the video
where the mime is showing you where on your

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:45.100
ID badge you can see your name spelled in
the character set used at the Games.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:49.340
So, some of these design challenges are certainly
not with us anymore.

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:53.470
We don't really have to face users who have
never seen a push-button telephone before.

00:04:53.470 --> 00:05:00.680
But others are with us, like for example how
to deal with a truly multi-national, international project.

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:05.370
Those things are still very difficult to do
today and have not actually been solved.

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:10.730
Three things that they did for their project
are the reasons why it was very successful.

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:15.050
A lot of athletes used this despite often
coming from very low-tech countries.

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:19.230
Remember, this was 1984 so it was very low-tech
at the time.

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:21.870
They had three design principles.

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:24.970
First was early focus on users.

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:26.740
Find out the users' characteristics.

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:30.530
Now, today we might call this persona development.

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:33.450
We have an entire day on that, today actually.

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:36.480
So they didn't use that word but they had
the same general idea.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:40.590
Observe users doing their normal tasks; field
studies are what we call this now.

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:46.260
Empirical measurements, test with real users,
start by testing what they called printed scenarios.

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:50.400
Maybe today we would call that paper prototyping,
but it was the same idea.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:54.940
It evolved to ever more elaborate prototypes
after that.

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:57.780
The third principle was iterative design.

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:02.090
You can never make the perfect user interface
the first time you try no matter how good

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:03.090
a designer you are.

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:07.230
You have to go through this cycle: design,
test, measure, redesign.

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:14.140
The more times you do that, the more times
you can drive up the quality of the final design.

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:19.060
In fact they went through 200 design iterations
for parts of their design.

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:22.480
Even today, this is a very impressive number.

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:27.500
But these three principles are essentially
what we teach today.

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:31.389
We use slightly different vocabulary but that's
the same thing we do today.

00:06:31.389 --> 00:06:36.970
So that's what I mean when I talk about immutable
characteristics of user experience.

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:39.560
These things keep being the same.

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:45.430
Now, despite these three principles being
really the foundation for quality design,

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:46.650
people didn't know about them.

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:53.230
They did a study while they were working on
the project where they asked several designers

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:59.600
and program managers to list what they called
the "main steps" to design systems.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:05.890
Only 16% mentioned all these three principles
despite them being so basic.

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:10.320
Early focus on users, empirical measurement,
and iterative design.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.490
Only 16% mentioned all three.

00:07:13.490 --> 00:07:19.900
In fact, more people -- 26% -- mentioned none
of these very basic things.

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:24.820
Now I would hope that if somebody were to
do this study again today they would get a

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:26.950
bigger percentage of people mentioning these
principles.

00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:31.780
But I actually think even today that it's
not the case that everybody actually would

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:32.780
mention all three.

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:38.760
And it's even more the case that not all projects
would follow all three principles, despite them

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:44.000
actually being truly basic, fundamental and required
for quality design.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:50.670
So we'll find that a lot of immutable rules
fall into this realm of user experience process.

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:56.730
Advice for good process tends to be the same
year after year.

00:07:56.730 --> 00:08:00.670
One of them is this point about testing with
real users.

00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:07.980
Here are two photos of a usability lab that
I used when I worked at Sun Microsystems in 1994.

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:14.210
But it could have been a photo of today. It's
the same idea: you put a computer, you put

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:18.150
a person with that computer, you have some
cameras, you have a one-way mirror, you have

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:21.680
a green plant in the corner.

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:26.300
Every usability lab has to have a green plant
in the corner!

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:29.750
And you have the back room where all of the
project team members sit there.

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:34.140
They are isolated from the user by the one-way
mirror so that the people on the team can

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:38.960
go "Oh NO! Why did they click the wrong button?"

00:08:38.969 --> 00:08:41.479
This happens year after year.

00:08:41.479 --> 00:08:45.881
The only way that you can tell that this is
an old photo is the computer itself.

00:08:45.881 --> 00:08:49.129
It's big and clunky because that's how they
looked back then.

00:08:49.129 --> 00:08:52.260
Here's a photo from a study that we did more
recently.

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:56.000
See, thinner computers, smaller cameras, but
the same basic idea.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.830
You sit next to the user and you observe what
they do.

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:00.970
You film it and you can look at the video
later.

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:06.450
A few more years later, even thinner computers
and even smaller cameras but the same setup.

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:10.829
I can keep taking these photos every year
and they will keep looking about the same.

00:09:10.829 --> 00:09:16.810
That said, there are certainly some things
that change the basic process of user testing.

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:19.370
So I'll show you a photo from a more recent
study we did.

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:23.660
This is a study that we did with eye-tracking
and mobile devices.

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:26.930
I should point out, first of all, that this
is not a real user.

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:33.930
This is one of our own experts who is playing
the user to try out the equipment.

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:38.889
Our study facilitator here who just gave the
intro - all of our test facilitators are too

00:09:38.889 --> 00:09:41.360
professional to actually laugh at the users.

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:42.720
We don't do that!

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:45.500
But we can laugh at each other.

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:51.889
This looked like a torture device, this thing,
but it was eye-tracking for mobile devices.

00:09:51.889 --> 00:09:53.959
This was one thing that we were trying out
at the time.

00:09:53.959 --> 00:09:56.889
So some things like that you do a little bit
differently.

00:09:56.889 --> 00:09:59.600
But basically, things remain the same year
after year after year.

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:05.990
I want to go back even further to the year 1628.

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:09.990
This is a photograph of the ship the Vasa,
which is a Swedish warship and it is in a

00:10:09.990 --> 00:10:10.990
museum in Stockholm.

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:16.220
You can go and see it today, it is a really
great museum and well worth seeing if you're in Stockholm.

00:10:16.220 --> 00:10:20.630
But in 1628 this was the biggest ship in the
Swedish navy and the biggest ship that had

00:10:20.630 --> 00:10:23.360
ever been built in Sweden.

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:27.720
So when they had just finished constructing
the ship and it had never been out sailing

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:33.040
yet, the newly appointed captain came aboard
the ship and said "We have got to test

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:34.569
the stability of this ship."

00:10:34.569 --> 00:10:39.019
He got all of the sailors on the deck and
got them to run back and forth.

00:10:39.019 --> 00:10:42.550
All of the sailors run to one side of the
ship and then all of the sailors run to the

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:43.829
other side of the ship.

00:10:43.829 --> 00:10:50.670
And as these guys run back and forth, the
ship started leaning more and more.

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:57.649
At this exact moment the admiral of the Swedish
navy comes down to the dock and orders the

00:10:57.649 --> 00:11:02.950
captain to stop the stability test because
this is the flagship of the navy.

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:07.740
It has to get out on the water - that's why
they built this ship!

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:10.389
Not to have the sailors run back and forth.

00:11:10.389 --> 00:11:14.389
So, the captain does as he's told and they
set sail.

00:11:14.389 --> 00:11:21.440
They sail out, leave the dock and reach the
middle of Stockholm harbor.

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:25.930
There's a gust of wind and the 
ship keels over.

00:11:25.930 --> 00:11:29.350
It sinks on the spot in the middle of the harbor.

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:32.009
This is why they have later recovered this
ship and it's in a museum.

00:11:32.009 --> 00:11:34.810
It's good for us, maybe, but it was bad for them.

00:11:34.810 --> 00:11:38.020
It was that unstable that it didn't even get
out of the harbor.

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:40.519
It was that unstable a ship!

00:11:40.519 --> 00:11:47.050
This story proves two things: the first thing
it proves is that Star Trek was right, captains

00:11:47.050 --> 00:11:51.339
are smarter than admirals.

00:11:51.339 --> 00:11:57.689
The second thing may be more relevant for
us which is: test before you launch.

00:11:57.689 --> 00:12:00.209
This is such a basic, basic point.

00:12:00.209 --> 00:12:07.839
But if you don't test and you just launch,
your ship or your product it has a big chance

00:12:07.839 --> 00:12:11.709
of catastrophe or failure in the marketplace.

00:12:11.709 --> 00:12:19.820
It's kind of like, right now there is a popular K-Pop
song says "You've got two choices, yes or yes."

00:12:19.829 --> 00:12:21.980
You can test or you can test.

00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:27.490
Your design will be tested by users, either
before you launch or after you launch.

00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:31.880
That's your only choice: yes or yes. Test or test.

00:12:31.900 --> 00:12:34.570
So that was the story about The Vasa.

00:12:34.570 --> 00:12:39.999
We have a lot of constants in the user experience
process because we are dealing with people,

00:12:39.999 --> 00:12:42.629
because we are dealing with some of the inherent
things about design.

00:12:42.629 --> 00:12:48.079
User-centered design works better than system-centered design or internally-focused design.

00:12:48.079 --> 00:12:50.759
Early focus on users works better than late
focus on users.

00:12:50.759 --> 00:12:53.029
This has been the same decade after decade.

00:12:53.029 --> 00:12:56.509
I predict that if I were going to do a talk
in ten years that I would say the exact same

00:12:56.509 --> 00:13:00.470
points because this is a fundamental, immutable rule.

00:13:00.470 --> 00:13:01.541
More iterations are better.

00:13:01.541 --> 00:13:05.149
You can never design the perfect interface
the first time you try.

00:13:05.149 --> 00:13:09.400
Every single iteration you do of the design,
you improve the quality.

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:15.910
It's better to have low-commitment, discount
usability methods and prototypes for the early

00:13:15.910 --> 00:13:20.180
iterations because you will discover design flaws.

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:24.709
In fact, there's only one rule about usability
testing which is that if you do a usability

00:13:24.709 --> 00:13:31.890
study and you discover nothing wrong with
the design, it proves you did the test wrong.

00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:35.269
Not that your design was perfect, because
that does not exist.

00:13:35.269 --> 00:13:39.220
So you will always discover ways of getting
it better, better, better and even better

00:13:39.220 --> 00:13:41.839
the more times you can do it.

00:13:41.839 --> 00:13:43.920
So you have to make those changes.

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:50.850
And it is easier to make that happen in a
company or an organization, given human nature,

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:56.589
if you haven't invested a lot -- both in terms
of resources but even more so in terms of

00:13:56.589 --> 00:14:02.369
commitment or emotional attachment to your
wonderful design idea -- if you've slaved

00:14:02.369 --> 00:14:06.750
over this for weeks, it's hard to be told
that no, it's wrong go and change it.

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:11.800
If you've worked on it for a day, that message
is more easily received and it's more likely

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:13.399
that the changes are made.

00:14:13.399 --> 00:14:21.069
And last, we have to look at both the experience
architecture (the big picture) and the details

00:14:21.069 --> 00:14:23.379
of the user interface on every screen.

00:14:23.379 --> 00:14:24.730
Both matter.

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:33.079
I've seen many cases where an otherwise wonderful
design sank to the bottom of the harbor because

00:14:33.079 --> 00:14:38.509
one button, one word was wrong and people
clicked the wrong button because these details

00:14:38.509 --> 00:14:42.899
matter so much for users' ability to use an
interface correctly.

00:14:42.899 --> 00:14:48.540
So that's why, one of the many reasons that
UX design is hard, is that you have got to

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:54.080
get both the architecture right and all the
details right as well or you will fail.

00:14:54.080 --> 00:15:01.490
I want to show you, as a little experiment, this piece of art and ask you what is this a picture of.

00:15:01.490 --> 00:15:07.949
I think most people would say this is a picture
of two boats or ships at sea.

00:15:07.949 --> 00:15:12.879
In fact, it's a Japanese artwork and the title
is The Torch Shrine in Oki Province.

00:15:12.879 --> 00:15:16.670
Now, where's the shrine?

00:15:16.670 --> 00:15:20.959
You may well think that this is a picture
of two boats but it is a picture of a shrine.

00:15:20.959 --> 00:15:21.959
Now, here's the shrine.

00:15:21.959 --> 00:15:27.839
If you look closely... very closely... you
can see the gate into that religious place.

00:15:27.839 --> 00:15:32.899
This is an artwork and the artist is playing with us.

00:15:32.899 --> 00:15:37.209
We start to think and get the wrong impression
of what this is supposed to be a picture of.

00:15:37.209 --> 00:15:40.059
This is acceptable if it's art.

00:15:40.059 --> 00:15:43.189
If it's design, it's not acceptable.

00:15:43.189 --> 00:15:45.679
There's a difference between art and design.

00:15:45.679 --> 00:15:49.929
Design has to have a functionality and you
have to be able to use it easily.

00:15:49.929 --> 00:15:56.340
Art you can ponder and think about and chuckle
over and be inspired by and all of these other things.

00:15:56.350 --> 00:15:58.040
Design has to work.

00:15:58.040 --> 00:16:00.880
So why is this the case? These are immutable rules.

00:16:00.889 --> 00:16:05.620
Things that are big and colorful and in the
foreground are more notable than things that

00:16:05.629 --> 00:16:08.749
are small and in the background and in muted colors.

00:16:08.749 --> 00:16:15.870
Honestly, those rules were true back in 1853
and they are true now and they will be true

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:18.260
in another hundred years. These are immutable rules.

00:16:18.260 --> 00:16:20.750
I want to go back even more in history.

00:16:20.750 --> 00:16:25.459
We'll go back to the year 750 BC to Athens, Greece.

00:16:25.459 --> 00:16:33.810
Here is a grave memorial -- a small section
of a bigger memorial -- and it shows the funeral.

00:16:33.810 --> 00:16:43.120
You can see the dead person lying there, you
see all the relatives tearing out their hair.

00:16:43.120 --> 00:16:48.340
Oh no! It's so terrible! And you feel that.

00:16:48.350 --> 00:16:53.690
If you look closely you will see that just
to the right of that dead person you will

00:16:53.690 --> 00:16:55.720
see a smaller person, probably their child.

00:16:55.720 --> 00:16:57.679
This is a person who lost one of their parents.

00:16:57.679 --> 00:17:01.959
This is a child who lost one of their parents.

00:17:01.959 --> 00:17:04.429
It's really a sad scene.

00:17:04.429 --> 00:17:08.890
Another thing to notice about that is that
if you look at the artistic style it is a

00:17:08.890 --> 00:17:10.330
primitive drawing style.

00:17:10.330 --> 00:17:15.180
This is a type of art style that makes some
people say that "Well, my seven-year-old nephew

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:17.200
could have drawn something better."

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:23.700
It turns out that most people's nephews are
not actually Picasso, but people think so anyway.

00:17:23.709 --> 00:17:26.580
It is a very primitive, simplistic drawing style.

00:17:26.580 --> 00:17:30.310
Let's go forward a few hundred years, still
staying in Athens, Greece.

00:17:30.310 --> 00:17:31.760
Here is another grave memorial.

00:17:31.760 --> 00:17:36.160
It is still old but it's not as old. It's from 400 BC.

00:17:36.170 --> 00:17:38.900
So we move from the Archaic Era to the Classic Era.

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:43.290
This is exactly why it's called the Classic
Era because this is classic art.

00:17:43.290 --> 00:17:47.130
This is the type of sculpture that has made
for thousands of years people say that the

00:17:47.130 --> 00:17:51.560
Greeks were an artistic peak and knew how
to make great statues.

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:56.170
It's a beautifully done, artistically done
marble piece.

00:17:56.170 --> 00:17:59.250
But what does it actually show?

00:17:59.250 --> 00:18:04.680
It's a grave memorial, but it shows the deceased
person as a young woman sitting there looking

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:08.790
in her jewelry chest that her maid is presenting.

00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:12.950
She is pondering what earrings to wear for
the party tonight.

00:18:12.950 --> 00:18:20.670
That's not a sad scene of death: it's refuting
the reality of what actually happened.

00:18:20.670 --> 00:18:24.020
We can analyze these two grave scenes at two
different levels.

00:18:24.020 --> 00:18:30.021
We can look at the surface design; the artistic
appearance of the picture and say that the

00:18:30.021 --> 00:18:33.530
previous one, the Archaic one was very primitive
in its drawing style.

00:18:33.530 --> 00:18:37.130
This is a very sophisticated depiction of a scene.

00:18:37.130 --> 00:18:45.400
Or we can look at the deep design and say
what is being shown in the old design is the

00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:52.200
reality of death and this is rejecting reality.

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:54.572
There's surface design and there is deep design.

00:18:54.572 --> 00:18:58.220
This was true more than 2,000 years ago and
it's true today as well.

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:01.200
We have got to consider both.

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:07.951
Another thing that is annoying to me about
today's world is that I think in the user

00:19:07.951 --> 00:19:13.590
experience field we have what I call vocabulary
inflation, which is people coming up with new

00:19:13.590 --> 00:19:17.520
words for the same thing constantly.

00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:22.190
It's super annoying, not just annoying but
it's also detrimental to the field because

00:19:22.190 --> 00:19:28.130
it leads to a lot of miscommunication of people
not understanding each other, people thinking

00:19:28.130 --> 00:19:30.600
that there are two different terms for two
different things.

00:19:30.600 --> 00:19:33.060
No, it's the same thing with two different names.

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:41.710
I'll show you some of the phrases used for
the general field throughout my career.

00:19:41.710 --> 00:19:45.340
We've used all of these different things to
refer to basically the same thing.

00:19:45.340 --> 00:19:50.190
There are little tweaks on them but this is
basically the same thing.

00:19:50.190 --> 00:19:53.600
This is true for a lot of more detailed elements as well.

00:19:53.600 --> 00:19:58.800
What you often find is that there will be
something that is 98% the same as the old

00:19:58.800 --> 00:20:04.260
thing and perhaps 2% different but it will
get a completely new name.

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:09.990
I would rather say that "This is that old,
well-known thing that you already know about,

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:13.020
but I would recommend that you change this
2% of it a little bit.

00:20:13.020 --> 00:20:14.990
That would probably be better for your project."

00:20:14.990 --> 00:20:16.160
But no, that's not what people do.

00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:21.170
They come up with a completely new, fanciful
term for what is basically the same thing

00:20:21.170 --> 00:20:22.170
over and over again.

00:20:22.170 --> 00:20:25.410
They keep re-inventing terms for the same thing.

00:20:25.410 --> 00:20:28.150
I think that there are many different reasons for that.

00:20:28.150 --> 00:20:33.680
One of them is that some people are ignorant
of the field and the history of the field

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:37.861
and they genuinely think that they have come
up with something new even though they are

00:20:37.861 --> 00:20:41.990
coming up with a small tweak on something
that is well-known that has been done for thirty years already.

00:20:41.990 --> 00:20:46.040
That's one reason for the new words, that
people don't know that they exist already.

00:20:46.040 --> 00:20:47.040
That's ignorance.

00:20:47.040 --> 00:20:54.430
Another reason is that the user experience
field is still a somewhat oppressed field.

00:20:54.430 --> 00:20:55.830
Not as much as it used to be.

00:20:55.830 --> 00:20:58.560
We have come a long way.

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:06.920
But still, in most companies, it is not the
most prestigious or the most well-funded discipline.

00:21:06.920 --> 00:21:13.560
So some people have the feeling that if they
just use a more fanciful vocabulary, if you

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:22.780
don't use the old, well-known words, but instead this new, fancy thing then that must be more important.

00:21:22.780 --> 00:21:29.410
The solution to a lack of respect is to come
up with a new fanciful vocabulary — but no.

00:21:29.410 --> 00:21:32.330
Using fancy words will not get you respect.

00:21:32.330 --> 00:21:35.060
Producing results will get you respect.

00:21:35.060 --> 00:21:40.150
That's what we should focus on rather than
always changing the names for the same things.

00:21:40.150 --> 00:21:45.460
A last reason for this vocabulary inflation
is that some people deliberately do it.

00:21:45.460 --> 00:21:51.990
They deliberately set out to make their own
name for something that is actually quite

00:21:51.990 --> 00:21:52.990
common.

00:21:52.990 --> 00:21:56.110
But they make a little tweak on it and now
it's a proprietary process.

00:21:56.110 --> 00:22:04.020
And if you only use Brand X name for something,
now your product will be successful even though

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:06.920
it is the same as always.

00:22:06.920 --> 00:22:11.930
I would much rather than companies would promote
themselves by saying that "We are more insightful,

00:22:11.930 --> 00:22:18.940
or deeper, or better at doing the things that
we have known for 30, 40 years."

00:22:18.950 --> 00:22:21.500
But that is often not the way that it is done.

00:22:21.500 --> 00:22:25.430
People like to have vocabulary inflation and
to invent their own terms, their own proprietary

00:22:25.430 --> 00:22:30.690
terms, leading to ever more confusion in our
sorry little field.

00:22:30.690 --> 00:22:32.000
That, I think, is sad.

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:38.870
Let's get rid of this vocabulary inflation
and keep a simple vocabulary instead.

00:22:38.870 --> 00:22:40.680
That would be much nicer.

00:22:40.680 --> 00:22:48.140
We also have a lot of immutables in the web
-- not just in the process but in the web itself.

00:22:48.140 --> 00:22:51.200
One of the big characteristics of the web
is that it's just big.

00:22:51.200 --> 00:22:55.880
The web is for all practical purposes infinitely
big in terms of the amount of information.

00:22:55.880 --> 00:23:01.930
There is about fifty billion pages on the
web, which means that if you were going to

00:23:01.930 --> 00:23:09.800
assign to yourself as your full-time job to
read the web that would take more than half a million years.

00:23:09.800 --> 00:23:12.860
Which is clearly not happening.

00:23:12.860 --> 00:23:13.860
People know that.

00:23:13.860 --> 00:23:15.330
Users know that.

00:23:15.330 --> 00:23:20.430
That's one of the reasons that they have developed
this very ruthless approach to screening information

00:23:20.430 --> 00:23:25.110
online: scanning rather than reading in-depth,
jumping between pages and this is also why

00:23:25.110 --> 00:23:26.110
they use search so much.

00:23:26.110 --> 00:23:32.010
It dives straight to where they want to go,
not where the company would like them to see.

00:23:32.010 --> 00:23:36.880
Not only is there so much stuff, but most
of it is bad.

00:23:36.880 --> 00:23:42.600
Most information is wrong or boring or it
is going to take you forever to dig out a

00:23:42.600 --> 00:23:45.770
small nugget of good stuff in there.

00:23:45.770 --> 00:23:46.890
Users know this also.

00:23:46.890 --> 00:23:54.180
This is one of the reasons why they're so
quick to leave a website at the smallest provocation.

00:23:54.180 --> 00:24:00.750
That's why it's so important to pay attention
to credibility, trust and persuasion guidelines.

00:24:00.750 --> 00:24:06.260
By the way, that's why we have a full day of that at
this conference.

00:24:06.260 --> 00:24:11.860
People are inherently skeptical of things
that they see on the web, they are not inherently very trusting.

00:24:11.870 --> 00:24:16.020
Because there is so much, it's easy to get
lost, which is why we have so many guidelines

00:24:16.020 --> 00:24:20.230
on helping people find their way and establish
their context.

00:24:20.230 --> 00:24:24.470
This is also why we have to reduce demands
on their short-term memory, which is also

00:24:24.470 --> 00:24:27.180
one of these immutable facts.

00:24:27.180 --> 00:24:32.550
People have a very limited amount of brain
capacity and it's not getting any bigger.

00:24:32.550 --> 00:24:35.800
People ten years from now will have about
the same short-term memory as today and we

00:24:35.800 --> 00:24:42.480
will have to retain all of the many design
guidelines that follow from these principles.

00:24:42.480 --> 00:24:47.231
There has been a nice study done by Microsoft
Research where they analyzed two billion page

00:24:47.231 --> 00:24:52.580
views and looked at how long time people
stayed on the page and therefore what is the

00:24:52.580 --> 00:24:58.550
probability of someone leaving a webpage after
a certain duration of a visit.

00:24:58.550 --> 00:25:02.140
What you can see is that the probability
of leaving is extremely high in the first

00:25:02.140 --> 00:25:08.190
few seconds. If you are one of the few webpages
where people actually stay for two minutes,

00:25:08.190 --> 00:25:15.100
two minutes is like eternity on the web, but if people stay on your webpage for

00:25:15.100 --> 00:25:20.550
two minutes then the probability of them leaving
right then is fairly low.

00:25:20.550 --> 00:25:24.250
The first twenty seconds or so are key.

00:25:24.250 --> 00:25:25.900
This is where the curve bends.

00:25:25.900 --> 00:25:32.860
In the first twenty seconds, you have to capture
people there, convince them that this is a

00:25:32.860 --> 00:25:37.600
credible, useful site to them or they are
going to be out of there.

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:41.640
People cannot read a very large number of
word count in twenty seconds so you have really

00:25:41.640 --> 00:25:43.880
got to be concise in your communication.

00:25:43.880 --> 00:25:53.580
We have two gaps in User Experience relative
to delivering what we should do.

00:25:53.580 --> 00:25:57.790
Sites and designs in general often fail on that.

00:25:57.790 --> 00:25:59.160
What we want is usefulness.

00:25:59.160 --> 00:26:02.300
That is to say, we want to actually do something
for our users.

00:26:02.300 --> 00:26:11.410
For a design to be useful it has to have two
quality parameters: utility and usability.

00:26:11.410 --> 00:26:15.640
Utility is a question of "Do you do something
that people need and want?"

00:26:15.640 --> 00:26:17.520
Usability is a question of "Can they use it?"

00:26:17.520 --> 00:26:24.210
The point is we need both.

00:26:24.210 --> 00:26:28.870
Bad utility means that you have something
that people people don't need.

00:26:28.870 --> 00:26:34.730
You could have the most wonderful user interface
design, but if the interface is designed to

00:26:34.730 --> 00:26:37.810
the wrong features, people are not going to use it.

00:26:37.810 --> 00:26:38.940
Why should they bother?

00:26:38.940 --> 00:26:43.320
Even if it's a great design, if it does the
wrong thing it's for nothing.

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:48.910
But at the same time, if you have great utility
you have all the right feature but people

00:26:48.910 --> 00:26:53.400
can't figure out how to use them, it's also
all for nothing.

00:26:53.400 --> 00:26:59.580
That feature that people can't figure out
how to use might as well not exist because

00:26:59.580 --> 00:27:02.780
you're not going to use it if you can't figure
out how to use it.

00:27:02.780 --> 00:27:06.100
That means that we need to satisfy both of
these two.

00:27:06.100 --> 00:27:13.750
This is why in design projects we often have
these two different gaps to overcome to deliver

00:27:13.750 --> 00:27:17.150
that goal of usefulness.

00:27:17.150 --> 00:27:20.830
The gap can be a utility gap or it can be
a usability gap.

00:27:20.830 --> 00:27:24.860
To illustrate that, I'm going to show you an example from a research project that we recently did.

00:27:24.870 --> 00:27:27.160
The details are published on
our website.

00:27:27.160 --> 00:27:33.120
We studied intelligent assistants: some of
the current AI assistants and voice assistants.

00:27:33.120 --> 00:27:40.140
These are things like Amazon's Alexa or Apple's
Siri where you can ask the assistant to do

00:27:40.140 --> 00:27:43.850
something and maybe it will give you a good answer.

00:27:43.850 --> 00:27:50.660
What we did is a study where the users were
all users of those types of systems and they

00:27:50.660 --> 00:27:55.190
were asked to record every time that they
had a need that they would like to get help

00:27:55.190 --> 00:27:57.390
on from their assistant.

00:27:57.390 --> 00:27:59.610
There was a long list of different user needs.

00:27:59.610 --> 00:28:07.610
We discovered that only in 7% of those cases did people actually get their problem solved by that assistant.

00:28:07.610 --> 00:28:11.260
This is terribly low!

00:28:11.260 --> 00:28:12.700
We had two other categories.

00:28:12.700 --> 00:28:20.570
In about a third of the cases, 34% of the
cases, is that gold area where AI can do it,

00:28:20.570 --> 00:28:23.360
however people did not choose to use it.

00:28:23.360 --> 00:28:28.020
Then we have a big category, which is 59%
where the current artificial intelligence

00:28:28.020 --> 00:28:32.000
is not up to the task and can't do 
what people want it to do.

00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:34.510
This is where I talk about those gaps.

00:28:34.510 --> 00:28:43.060
The utility gap, which is that top box, is where the tasks can't be done at all -- the utility is just not there.

00:28:43.060 --> 00:28:50.380
The middle area, the usability gap, the computer
could do it but users do not do it because

00:28:50.380 --> 00:28:54.631
it is too difficult to do, too cumbersome
to use, or some other usability problem explains

00:28:54.631 --> 00:28:55.980
why people don't do it.

00:28:55.980 --> 00:29:01.030
If you look at these two, you might think that it looks like the utility gap is the most serious.

00:29:01.030 --> 00:29:05.240
It's the biggest for sure and so that is where
we should focus our investments on building

00:29:05.240 --> 00:29:06.800
better artificial intelligence.

00:29:06.800 --> 00:29:09.750
But let's look at this a little more carefully.

00:29:09.750 --> 00:29:15.300
Let's look right now at things that are possible
to do with today's technology.

00:29:15.300 --> 00:29:18.190
That's what was the two bottom boxes on the previous bar chart.

00:29:18.190 --> 00:29:22.860
I used the same color coding so green is the
tasks that people actually did and that was

00:29:22.860 --> 00:29:24.740
7% as the percent of all tasks.

00:29:24.740 --> 00:29:31.430
Let's look at only what's possible with today's
artificial intelligence technology: it's 17% of those tasks.

00:29:31.430 --> 00:29:40.720
And what's possible but not done is 34% of
all tasks but 83% of the possible tasks, things

00:29:40.720 --> 00:29:44.920
that could already hypothetically be done. That's huge.

00:29:44.920 --> 00:29:49.640
The usability gap is actually a huge percentage
of what's possible.

00:29:49.640 --> 00:29:54.700
Now I want to do an exercise where we'll do
a hypothetical projection into the future.

00:29:54.700 --> 00:29:59.960
This is the data from the current state of
the art based on the study that we just did this year.

00:29:59.970 --> 00:30:02.760
Let's assume we had perfect artificial intelligence.

00:30:02.760 --> 00:30:10.060
We have done so much work in AI that now the
computer can do every possible thing that users want.

00:30:10.060 --> 00:30:13.150
This of course will take some time, but let's
assume that that is done.

00:30:13.150 --> 00:30:19.130
Let's make one more assumption which is that
the usability of the new features is the same

00:30:19.130 --> 00:30:21.630
as the usability of the old features.

00:30:21.630 --> 00:30:25.110
I'll admit that this is just an assumption
because we are just doing a scenario for the

00:30:25.110 --> 00:30:29.530
future, but I think that this is a realistic
assumption that usability of the new features

00:30:29.530 --> 00:30:32.110
is the same as usability of the old features.

00:30:32.110 --> 00:30:35.220
This means that the chart will now look like this.

00:30:35.220 --> 00:30:43.130
New features that become possible are now
colored either green or gold in the same proportion

00:30:43.130 --> 00:30:45.110
as the old features.

00:30:45.110 --> 00:30:54.940
Now we can re-organize the groups together
and we will see that the area that represents tasks actually done has

00:30:54.940 --> 00:30:57.460
now grown to 17%.

00:30:57.460 --> 00:31:02.260
This means that we have had an increase in
tasks done by 10 percentage points.

00:31:02.260 --> 00:31:05.270
We go from 7 to 17.

00:31:05.270 --> 00:31:06.730
This is actually great!

00:31:06.730 --> 00:31:10.120
We more than double so this is worth doing.

00:31:10.120 --> 00:31:18.900
But at the same time we see 83% not done because
of that usability gap if we assume that new

00:31:18.900 --> 00:31:22.470
features have the same bad usability as today's systems.

00:31:22.470 --> 00:31:31.950
That's what leads me to say that the usability
gap is enormously serious for getting these

00:31:31.950 --> 00:31:34.990
types of products really used more in the future.

00:31:34.990 --> 00:31:44.630
We have to make artificial intelligence more
suited for human behavior so that people will

00:31:44.630 --> 00:31:49.300
both be able to do the tasks and want to do
the tasks with their assistant.

00:31:49.300 --> 00:31:50.930
That is crucial!

00:31:50.930 --> 00:31:53.240
Crucial to make that happen.

00:31:53.240 --> 00:31:58.610
I want to show you a longitudinal data chart, which is very rare that people do this,

00:31:58.610 --> 00:32:06.460
but for many years we collected user satisfaction
data where every time we studied a website

00:32:06.460 --> 00:32:12.610
we would ask the test users to rate on a 1
to 7 scale their satisfaction.

00:32:12.610 --> 00:32:19.350
It was subjective satisfaction, with 7 being
very satisfied and 1 being very dissatisfied.

00:32:19.350 --> 00:32:27.740
On this chart here each dot represents one
test of one website and they are sorted by years

00:32:27.740 --> 00:32:32.440
from the very old studies to the more recent studies. What does this data show?

00:32:32.440 --> 00:32:35.080
It shows that there is no trend.

00:32:35.080 --> 00:32:39.590
I can't do any data analysis that shows that
by X percent per year there's a change...

00:32:39.590 --> 00:32:41.570
There's no change.

00:32:41.570 --> 00:32:44.620
There's no change in user satisfaction year over year.

00:32:44.620 --> 00:32:53.740
Let me first ask you: is it the case that
websites today are just as bad as websites

00:32:53.740 --> 00:32:55.490
were during the dot-com bubble?

00:32:55.490 --> 00:32:57.580
No, this is not right.

00:32:57.580 --> 00:32:58.580
This is not the case.

00:32:58.580 --> 00:33:02.950
Websites today definitely are much better
than they used to be.

00:33:02.950 --> 00:33:08.930
I worked on these things back then and they were terrible!

00:33:08.930 --> 00:33:14.220
Today's websites still have their problems
but they were really terrible, really bad.

00:33:14.220 --> 00:33:18.010
There was only one good thing about websites
back during the dot-com bubble and that's

00:33:18.010 --> 00:33:21.110
that it was very easy to be a usability consultant.

00:33:21.110 --> 00:33:30.160
They were so bad that being a usability consultant
in those days was like shooting fish in a barrel.

00:33:30.160 --> 00:33:33.275
You just had to look at them for a very short amount
of time and then you would be able to say

00:33:33.275 --> 00:33:37.190
"Oh, you can double your business by stopping
these five stupidities that you are doing

00:33:37.190 --> 00:33:39.400
that are preventing people from using your website!"

00:33:39.400 --> 00:33:43.100
Sites are definitely better today.

00:33:43.100 --> 00:33:49.170
So why is satisfaction not up when quality
is honestly up a lot?

00:33:49.170 --> 00:33:55.210
That's because subjective satisfaction is
not a matter of the quality of the delivered

00:33:55.210 --> 00:33:57.700
product or experience.

00:33:57.700 --> 00:34:04.120
Satisfaction is a matter of the relationship
between what you get relative to what you expect.

00:34:04.120 --> 00:34:11.230
It's very expectation-driven and what happens
is that users' expectation for the quality

00:34:11.230 --> 00:34:18.549
of your website is based on their collective experience
of all the other websites they use.

00:34:18.549 --> 00:34:20.859
That's what forms people's expectations.

00:34:20.859 --> 00:34:25.759
What happens is websites are getting much
better and that means that people's experiences

00:34:25.759 --> 00:34:28.700
using these other websites are much better.

00:34:28.700 --> 00:34:32.990
That means that their expectations of 
your website are now much higher which means

00:34:32.990 --> 00:34:37.389
that their satisfaction is not going to get
any better even if you improve the site.

00:34:37.389 --> 00:34:42.339
In fact, if you are one of those companies
that does collect customer satisfaction data

00:34:42.339 --> 00:34:46.700
year after year and your satisfaction score
this year is the same as your satisfaction

00:34:46.700 --> 00:34:51.409
score last year, this proves that you did a good job.

00:34:51.409 --> 00:34:55.839
Stagnant customer satisfaction data means
that you have done a good job in improving

00:34:55.839 --> 00:34:59.640
the quality of your design to keep up with
user expectations.

00:34:59.640 --> 00:35:04.950
Now, if you have a higher customer satisfaction
score then that doesn't indicate a good job

00:35:04.950 --> 00:35:07.279
but a great, exceptional job.

00:35:07.279 --> 00:35:11.799
You've improved enormously, not just improved
like other people.

00:35:11.799 --> 00:35:17.640
But this is one of those immutable rules of
user experience: user satisfaction is not

00:35:17.640 --> 00:35:25.529
really going to go up because demands or expectations
are going to increase comparatively with what

00:35:25.529 --> 00:35:27.920
the entire rest of the world is doing.

00:35:27.920 --> 00:35:34.269
That means that we constantly have that job
of continuous improvement, continuous quality

00:35:34.269 --> 00:35:38.250
improvement is one of those immutable rules
of user experience.

00:35:38.250 --> 00:35:43.249
We have to keep getting better because the
customers are demanding that we get better

00:35:43.249 --> 00:35:47.339
or they will just abandon doing business with
our website.

00:35:47.339 --> 00:35:48.339
That's another immutable rule.

00:35:48.339 --> 00:35:50.720
A lot of these things just keep being very,
very constant.

00:35:50.720 --> 00:35:59.180
I want to close with what I think is the biggest
constant of them all which is that UX is people.

00:35:59.180 --> 00:36:05.390
It's not technology, it's not really about
computers, it's about people.

00:36:05.390 --> 00:36:08.090
It's about people in two ways.

00:36:08.090 --> 00:36:11.680
It's about people in terms of the users.

00:36:11.680 --> 00:36:15.480
It's about the people who are actually using
our design and we have to design for them,

00:36:15.480 --> 00:36:18.830
for their characteristics, which often are
not changing very much.

00:36:18.830 --> 00:36:20.660
That's one way in which UX is people.

00:36:20.660 --> 00:36:24.910
There's another way which is not talked about
quite as much which is that it's people in

00:36:24.910 --> 00:36:27.200
the sense of the design team of us.

00:36:27.200 --> 00:36:31.330
It's the people who are working on these projects.

00:36:31.330 --> 00:36:37.849
That's why these process issues are both very important
but also tend to be about the same because

00:36:37.849 --> 00:36:43.840
all of these criteria for how you go about
designing a good user experience.

00:36:43.840 --> 00:36:49.500
They tend to be about the same because UX
design is incredibly complicated.

00:36:49.500 --> 00:36:52.920
There are so many different details that combine
in so many different ways.

00:36:52.920 --> 00:36:57.270
You've got to get them all right and this
is not something that you can do by just sitting

00:36:57.270 --> 00:36:59.059
there waiting for inspiration to strike.

00:36:59.059 --> 00:37:01.040
And then it springs out of your brain!

00:37:01.040 --> 00:37:07.650
And then here is a wonderful website or artificially
intelligent agent or whatever...

00:37:07.650 --> 00:37:10.640
The first idea is never going to be the best design.

00:37:10.640 --> 00:37:20.099
This is why we have to think about the human
characteristics of us, of our inability to

00:37:20.099 --> 00:37:22.549
be perfect, our humanity as well.

00:37:22.549 --> 00:37:24.410
So that's how UX is about people.

00:37:24.410 --> 00:37:29.440
We have that duality in many, many ways, and
I've hinted at some of them throughout this talk.

00:37:29.450 --> 00:37:33.440
We have to talk about utility and usability.

00:37:33.450 --> 00:37:40.250
We have to consider the surface design -- is
it beautiful? -- and the deep design, the

00:37:40.250 --> 00:37:42.789
more true experience.

00:37:42.789 --> 00:37:47.769
All of these things have to be done, 
the architecture and the user interface, tiny

00:37:47.769 --> 00:37:50.599
details about the buttons and so forth; all
of these things.

00:37:50.599 --> 00:37:57.680
There's a lot of duality to user experience
products, which is why we have to have good,

00:37:57.680 --> 00:38:01.960
solid processes in place to make sure that
we cover that gamut and that we do good work

00:38:01.960 --> 00:38:04.090
on multiple levels at the same time.

00:38:04.090 --> 00:38:08.529
That's why it's a complicated thing to do.

00:38:08.529 --> 00:38:13.109
The only good news about it is that it's such a complicated thing to get right so that we certainly will

00:38:13.109 --> 00:38:16.910
have job security forever.

00:38:16.910 --> 00:38:21.680
I don't think that I'll ever give a speech
that says "User experience problems are solved

00:38:21.680 --> 00:38:23.840
and we can all go home!"

00:38:23.840 --> 00:38:26.900
But it's a long-term project.

00:38:26.900 --> 00:38:29.730
We've come a long way but there is an even
further long way to go.

00:38:29.730 --> 00:38:34.630
But if you just remember one thing, take away
one thing back and hammer it in to your teammates

00:38:34.630 --> 00:38:37.950
and the rest of your company it's this one message.

00:38:37.950 --> 00:38:39.290
UX is people.

00:38:39.290 --> 00:38:40.480
Thank you very much!

